{"step_index":624,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-16T19:53:27Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-16T21:53:27+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-10-16T21:53:27+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///home/davidel/.gemini/antigravity/brain/335ea41a-df46-4ffa-bb4c-eefc5a5248cd/thesis_notes.md`\nTotal Lines: 97\nTotal Bytes: 6949\nShowing lines 1 to 97\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # BlueAgent Empirical Thesis Notes & Benchmark Analysis\n2: \n3: This document compiles the quantitative evaluation results, methodology design decisions, and architectural insights for the thesis on **BlueAgent: Multi-Tenant Cyber Range Verification and Isolation with Incus and Open Virtual Network (OVN)**.\n4: \n5: ---\n6: \n7: ## 1. ZFS Copy-on-Write (CoW) Ephemeral Sandboxing (Core Thesis Contribution)\n8: \n9: ### 1.1 The Challenge of State Verification in Live CTF Infrastructure\n10: In Attack-Defense and Red/Blue Cyber Range competitions, teams deploy patches, configuration changes, or service hardening. When the verification engine tests challenge functionality, executing destructive or state-altering verifier scripts directly on the production challenge containers risks corrupting team state or causing cascading outages.\n11: \n12: BlueAgent solves this through **ephemeral sandboxing**:\n13: 1. When a team submits or automated health checking runs, BlueAgent snapshots each running container in the team's challenge.\n14: 2. An ephemeral copy of each container is cloned into an isolated sandbox logical network using `incus copy --instance-only`.\n15: 3. Verification scripts execute against the cloned instances in the sandbox.\n16: 4. Upon test completion, the ephemeral clones and isolated OVN network are immediately destroyed.\n17: \n18: ### 1.2 Quantitative Speedup Factor: Deploy Create vs. CoW Clone\n19: Because BlueAgent is backed by a **ZFS storage pool** (`blueagent-zfs`), container cloning uses ZFS Copy-on-Write block pointer reference\n<truncated 3804 bytes>\nlidation $\\to$ Incus Instance Creation $\\to$ Config File Push $\\to$ Start $\\to$ Runtime Network Attachment $\\to$ SQLite Transaction.\n72:      - **Verify:** OVN Ephemeral Network Creation $\\to$ CoW Clone Creation $\\to$ Network Interface Rebinding $\\to$ Clone Start $\\to$ Verifier Runner Execution $\\to$ Verification Commands $\\to$ Ephemeral Resource Teardown.\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: ## 3. Asymmetric Scaling: $M$ Teams vs. $N$ Containers per Team\n77: \n78: A vital finding from comparing equal-density workloads ($M=5, N=10$ vs. $M=10, N=5$, both totaling 50 live / 100 peak containers):\n79: \n80: | Metric | $M=5, N=10$ (Fewer Teams, Dense Challenges) | $M=10, N=5$ (More Teams, Lean Challenges) | Dominant Bottleneck |\n81: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n82: | **Deploy Wall-Clock** | 56.40s | 68.25s | Host process scheduling ($M=10$) |\n83: | **Deploy Create Avg** | 46.53s | 52.67s | Incus API daemon concurrent requests |\n84: | **Verify Wall-Clock** | 29.58s | 26.36s | Clone & boot concurrency |\n85: | **CoW Clone Avg** | 20.15s | 14.17s | Per-sandbox clone batching ($N=10$ vs $N=5$) |\n86: | **OVN Network Create Avg** | 1.13s | 2.50s | OVN Central database write lock |\n87: | **Verify Cleanup Avg** | 3.19s | 3.86s | OVN logical switch deletion |\n88: \n89: - **OVN Central Contention:** As the number of independent teams $M$ scales, OVN Northbound/Southbound database transactions become the primary networking factor (`ovn_network_create_sec` increases from 1.13s at $M=5$ to 2.50s at $M=10$).\n90: - **Per-Team Parallelism:** When $N$ is large ($N=10$), individual team verify calls must snapshot and clone 10 containers, making per-team verification slightly longer (20.15s vs 14.17s), but placing less lock contention on OVN.\n91: \n92: ---\n93: \n94: ## 4. Heavy Image (`ubuntu-heavy`) Comparison (Work in Progress)\n95: \n96: *(This section will be populated with empirical comparative data upon completion of the Ubuntu 24.04 `ubuntu-heavy` benchmark runs).*\n97: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":625,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-16T19:53:27Z","input_tokens":2918,"cache_read_tokens":206622,"output_tokens":4827,"thinking":"The **6-Barrier Synchronized Evaluation Methodology** requires distinct Setup, Start, and Cleanup phases. This separation guarantees all containers ($2 \\times M \\times N$) are in a consistent \"at rest\" state concurrently prior to cleanup, critical for synchronized evaluation. A 100ms Subprocess Telemetry captures CPU, RAM, and ZFS ARC metrics. This isolates performance insights from any post-barrier polling skew.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"\"# BlueAgent Empirical Thesis Notes & Benchmark Analysis\\n\\nThis document compiles the quantitative evaluation results, methodology design decisions, and architectural insights for the thesis on **BlueAgent: Multi-Tenant Cyber Range Verification and Isolation with Incus and Open Virtual Network (OVN)**.\\n\\nAll benchmarks were executed on an **AMD Ryzen 7 7700X (8 physical cores / 16 threads), 15 GiB RAM, 2 GiB swap**, running Linux 6.8 with Incus 6.0 and ZFS on Linux (`blueagent-zfs` storage pool).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. ZFS Copy-on-Write (CoW) Ephemeral Sandboxing (Core Thesis Contribution)\\n\\n### 1.1 The Challenge of State Verification in Live CTF Infrastructure\\nIn Attack-Defense and Red/Blue Cyber Range competitions, teams deploy patches, configuration changes, or service hardening. When the verification engine tests challenge functionality, executing destructive or state-altering verifier scripts directly on the production challenge containers risks corrupting team state or causing cascading outages.\\n\\nBlueAgent solves this through **ephemeral sandboxing**:\\n1. When a team submits or automated health checking runs, BlueAgent snapshots each running container in the team's challenge.\\n2. An ephemeral copy of each container is cloned into an isolated sandbox logical network using `incus copy --instance-only`.\\n3. Verification scripts execute against the cloned instances in the sandbox.\\n4. Upon test completion, the ephemeral clones and isolated OVN network are immediately destroyed.\\n\\n### 1.2 Quantitative Speedup Factor: Deploy Create vs. CoW Clone\\nBecause BlueAgent is backed by a **ZFS storage pool** (`blueagent-zfs`), container cloning uses ZFS Copy-on-Write block pointer references rather than block-level data copies. While creating an initial container instance (`Deploy Create`) requires unpacking and initializing filesystem structures from the base image, cloning a running instance (`CoW Clone`) is an $O(1)$ metadata transaction.\\n\\nAs system concurrency scales, the performance advantage of ZFS CoW cloning bec\n<truncated 10255 bytes>","Description":"\"Update thesis notes with full comparative analysis, exact empirical numbers, and tables for Alpine vs. Ubuntu Heavy\"","Overwrite":"true","TargetFile":"\"/home/davidel/.gemini/antigravity/brain/335ea41a-df46-4ffa-bb4c-eefc5a5248cd/thesis_notes.md\"","toolAction":"\"Updating thesis_notes.md\"","toolSummary":"\"Update thesis_notes.md with complete empirical findings\""}}],"truncated_fields":["tool_calls"]}
