{"step_index":635,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-16T19:58:26Z","input_tokens":219637,"cache_read_tokens":0,"output_tokens":959,"content":"Include these **5 graphs** in your thesis evaluation chapter:\n\n1. **Cold Deploy Create vs. ZFS CoW Clone Latency (Grouped Bar or Dual-Line Chart)**\n   - **Data:** `deploy_create_avg_sec` vs. `cow_clone_avg_sec` from [`thesis_alpine_results.csv`](file:///home/davidel/.gemini/antigravity/brain/335ea41a-df46-4ffa-bb4c-eefc5a5248cd/scratch/thesis_alpine_results.csv) and [`thesis_ubuntu_results.csv`](file:///home/davidel/.gemini/antigravity/brain/335ea41a-df46-4ffa-bb4c-eefc5a5248cd/scratch/thesis_ubuntu_results.csv).\n   - **What it proves:** Your core contribution—ZFS CoW cloning achieves up to a **$3.6\\times$ speedup** ($14.1\\text{s}$ vs. $50.6\\text{s}$ on Alpine; $22.2\\text{s}$ vs. $76.2\\text{s}$ on Ubuntu) and remains $O(1)$ regardless of image size (3 MB vs. 1.5 GB).\n\n2. **Micro-Phase Latency Breakdown at Scale (Stacked Bar Chart)**\n   - **Data:** Micro-phase columns in `*_results.csv` across $M \\in \\{2, 5, 10\\}$ (fixed $N=5$).\n   - **What it proves:** Where time is actually spent inside `Deploy` (*Create, Push, Start, Net*) and `Verify Setup` (*OVN Switch Creation, CoW Clone, NIC Rebind, Boot*), showing how OVN switch creation scales with $M$ while cloning scales with $M \\times N$.\n\n3. **Net Container Memory Scaling: Alpine vs. Ubuntu Heavy (Line Chart)**\n   - **Data:** `steady_peak_net_delta_mb` vs. `peak_verify_containers` ($2 \\to 100$ containers) from `*_results.csv`.\n   - **What it proves:** Linear, predictable memory scaling (`~15.3 MB/container` for Alpine vs. `~52.5 MB/container` for Ubuntu Heavy), demonstrating that 100 heavy `systemd` containers fit in ~5.2 GB of net RAM without swap.\n\n4. **Single-Scenario Lifecycle Timeline: CPU % and Net RAM over Time (Dual-Axis Time-Series Plot)**\n   - **Data:** `elapsed_sec`, `cpu_pct`, and `net_ram_mb` for `Tier M=10 N=5` from [`thesis_ubuntu_timeseries.csv`](file:///home/davidel/.gemini/antigravity/brain/335ea41a-df46-4ffa-bb4c-eefc5a5248cd/scratch/thesis_ubuntu_timeseries.csv) (with vertical dashed lines marking the 6 barriers).\n   - **What it proves:** Visually shows the 6 phases in action—RAM stepping up after Deploy (50 containers), doubling during Verify Setup (100 containers), and returning cleanly to baseline after Teardown with zero memory leaks.\n\n5. **Per-Step CPU & I/O Wait Utilization (Grouped Bar Chart)**\n   - **Data:** `cpu_avg_pct`, `cpu_max_pct`, and `iowait_avg_pct` per `step_name` from [`thesis_ubuntu_step_resources.csv`](file:///home/davidel/.gemini/antigravity/brain/335ea41a-df46-4ffa-bb4c-eefc5a5248cd/scratch/thesis_ubuntu_step_resources.csv).\n   - **What it proves:** That the host CPU remains well below saturation (~20–23% average across 8 cores) even during 100-container bursts."}
